1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,432
Part two: the General Health Questionnaire.

2
00:00:04,632 --> 00:00:09,527
You answer its key questions from the patient's records in PointClickCare.

3
00:00:11,427 --> 00:00:14,607
You don't answer every question in this questionnaire.

4
00:00:14,907 --> 00:00:23,599
Skip overall physical health, instrumental activities, understanding of health conditions, and everything after the diet question.

5
00:00:23,899 --> 00:00:31,076
Answer five: activities of daily living, falls, pain, life planning documents, and recommended diet.

6
00:00:31,376 --> 00:00:34,177
You'll find each answer in PointClickCare.

7
00:00:36,177 --> 00:00:40,361
The initial assessment opens on the General Health Questionnaire.

8
00:00:40,611 --> 00:00:44,098
It has many questions, but you don't answer all of them.

9
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,754
The first question is: How would you rate your overall physical health?

10
00:00:52,004 --> 00:00:55,230
You don't answer it. Leave it blank, and move on.

11
00:00:56,544 --> 00:01:04,127
The second question asks: What activities of daily living (ADLs) do you currently require assistance with?

12
00:01:04,377 --> 00:01:10,402
The options are walking, feeding, toileting, transferring, bathing, grooming, and dressing.

13
00:01:10,652 --> 00:01:15,035
You answer it from the patient's MDS, in PointClickCare.

14
00:01:16,344 --> 00:01:21,635
The MDS (Minimum Data Set) is an assessment of the patient's basic functions

15
00:01:21,675 --> 00:01:24,841
that the facility completes regularly.

16
00:01:25,091 --> 00:01:29,964
Use the most recent one, so your answers match the patient's latest data.

17
00:01:30,214 --> 00:01:34,719
On the patient's page in PointClickCare, click the MDS tab.

18
00:01:35,777 --> 00:01:39,154
The assessments are listed by date, newest first.

19
00:01:39,404 --> 00:01:44,768
The latest one is the annual assessment from 10/7/2026.

20
00:01:45,018 --> 00:01:49,779
You can open it with view, or as a printout. Printing is the easier way.

21
00:01:50,029 --> 00:01:53,238
Click reports, next to the latest assessment.

22
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:57,492
A Print Options window opens.

23
00:01:57,742 --> 00:02:01,357
Under Select print options, check MDS Form.

24
00:02:02,726 --> 00:02:04,378
Then click Print.

25
00:02:06,044 --> 00:02:10,869
The MDS form opens as a PDF, in a new browser tab.

26
00:02:12,377 --> 00:02:16,814
Before you open the MDS, here's how to read its function scores.

27
00:02:17,114 --> 00:02:22,662
06 means independent: the patient completes the activity alone, with no help.

28
00:02:22,702 --> 00:02:25,379
Leave that activity unchecked.

29
00:02:25,679 --> 00:02:29,511
Any other code means the patient needs assistance,

30
00:02:29,551 --> 00:02:33,923
from 05, setup or clean-up, down to 01, dependent.

31
00:02:34,223 --> 00:02:39,720
Activities that weren't attempted, such as code 88, also count as needing assistance.

32
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:42,019
Check them in ThoroughCare.

33
00:02:44,019 --> 00:02:49,164
Back in ThoroughCare, take the activities one at a time, starting with walking.

34
00:02:49,419 --> 00:02:51,754
Switch to the MDS form.

35
00:02:52,004 --> 00:02:55,085
Press Ctrl + F, and search for walk 10.

36
00:02:57,119 --> 00:03:01,438
Step through the matches to Section GG, Functional Abilities.

37
00:03:01,688 --> 00:03:09,121
Here are three walking items: walk 10 feet, walk 50 feet with two turns, and walk 150 feet.

38
00:03:09,371 --> 00:03:16,591
Walk 10 feet is coded 88: not attempted, due to a medical condition or safety concerns.

39
00:03:16,841 --> 00:03:23,034
Because of that code, walk 50 feet and walk 150 feet are skipped, and left blank.

40
00:03:23,284 --> 00:03:27,923
The code isn't 06, so the patient needs assistance with walking.

41
00:03:28,176 --> 00:03:30,745
So in ThoroughCare, check Walking.

42
00:03:36,319 --> 00:03:39,448
You can also search for any activity by name.

43
00:03:39,698 --> 00:03:42,058
For example, search for bath.

44
00:03:47,086 --> 00:03:50,978
Step through the matches until you reach the self-care scores.

45
00:03:51,228 --> 00:03:56,123
Here, eating is scored 05: setup or clean-up assistance.

46
00:03:56,373 --> 00:04:00,734
It isn't 06, so the patient needs assistance with eating.

47
00:04:00,984 --> 00:04:03,316
In ThoroughCare, check Feeding.

48
00:04:06,252 --> 00:04:10,933
Back in the MDS, toileting hygiene is 01: dependent.

49
00:04:13,114 --> 00:04:14,886
So check Toileting.

50
00:04:19,886 --> 00:04:25,015
Shower/bathe self is also 01, so the patient needs assistance.

51
00:04:25,267 --> 00:04:26,756
Check Bathing.

52
00:04:29,086 --> 00:04:33,981
Grooming depends on two items: oral hygiene, and personal hygiene.

53
00:04:34,247 --> 00:04:41,702
Oral hygiene is 04: supervision or touching assistance. Personal hygiene is 01.

54
00:04:44,881 --> 00:04:47,813
Both need assistance, so check Grooming.

55
00:04:50,186 --> 00:04:55,443
Dressing also has two items: upper body dressing, and lower body dressing.

56
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:04,143
Upper body dressing is 01.

57
00:05:04,393 --> 00:05:07,469
Lower body dressing is 01 too.

58
00:05:08,532 --> 00:05:12,309
The patient needs assistance with both, so check Dressing.

59
00:05:16,357 --> 00:05:18,585
Last, search for transfer.

60
00:05:22,619 --> 00:05:25,743
Step through the matches to the mobility items.

61
00:05:25,993 --> 00:05:30,213
Three items show whether the patient needs help with transfers:

62
00:05:30,253 --> 00:05:35,014
sit to stand, chair/bed-to-chair transfer, and toilet transfer.

63
00:05:35,264 --> 00:05:40,304
Sit to stand and chair/bed-to-chair transfer are 01: dependent.

64
00:05:40,344 --> 00:05:43,828
Toilet transfer is 88: not attempted.

65
00:05:44,085 --> 00:05:50,025
Remember the scale at the top: 06 means independent, with no help from a helper.

66
00:05:50,723 --> 00:05:55,511
Any other code means the patient needs assistance. So check Transferring.

67
00:05:58,852 --> 00:06:02,573
Now every activity the patient needs help with is checked.

68
00:06:02,823 --> 00:06:08,296
Skip the next question: What instrumental activities of daily living (IADLs)

69
00:06:08,336 --> 00:06:11,810
do you currently require assistance with?

70
00:06:13,119 --> 00:06:17,928
Next: Do you have a history of falling, or feeling unsteady while walking?

71
00:06:24,191 --> 00:06:27,656
Answer it from the MDS. Search for fall.

72
00:06:27,906 --> 00:06:31,051
Step through the matches to J1800.

73
00:06:31,301 --> 00:06:38,393
It asks: Has the resident had any falls since admission, entry or reentry, or the prior assessment?

74
00:06:38,643 --> 00:06:44,071
0 means No, and 1 means Yes. Here, the code is 0: no falls.

75
00:06:48,104 --> 00:06:49,704
So answer No.

76
00:06:52,252 --> 00:06:55,163
Next: Do you have any problems with pain?

77
00:06:55,413 --> 00:07:06,174
There are three answers: No, I do not experience pain routinely. Yes, my pain is currently adequately managed. And yes, my pain is currently unmanaged.

78
00:07:06,424 --> 00:07:10,444
Answer it from the patient's pain scores, in PointClickCare.

79
00:07:10,831 --> 00:07:15,439
The patient's banner shows the latest pain score. Here, it's zero.

80
00:07:25,052 --> 00:07:29,243
But one score isn't enough. You need the history of the pain scores.

81
00:07:29,499 --> 00:07:31,919
Open the Weights and Vitals tab.

82
00:07:36,052 --> 00:07:38,782
Find Pain Level, and click view all.

83
00:07:40,719 --> 00:07:45,635
The Pain Level Summary lists every pain score from the last 90 days.

84
00:07:45,886 --> 00:07:51,143
Here, almost every reading is zero. The patient doesn't have pain routinely.

85
00:07:52,454 --> 00:07:56,645
So answer: No, I do not experience pain routinely.

86
00:07:58,086 --> 00:08:00,911
What if the pain scores aren't always zero?

87
00:08:01,286 --> 00:08:07,290
If they go up and down, for example five, seven, eight, or nine, the patient has pain.

88
00:08:07,552 --> 00:08:15,028
Then choose between: Yes, my pain is currently adequately managed, and Yes, my pain is currently unmanaged.

89
00:08:16,352 --> 00:08:19,263
To decide, go back to the MDS.

90
00:08:19,520 --> 00:08:24,287
Search for pain, and find J0100: Pain Management.

91
00:08:27,152 --> 00:08:34,436
It asks whether, at any time in the last five days, the patient received a scheduled pain medication regimen,

92
00:08:34,686 --> 00:08:40,690
received PRN (as-needed) pain medication, or was offered and declined it,

93
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:46,133
or received a non-medication intervention for pain, such as physical therapy.

94
00:08:46,383 --> 00:08:49,894
For each one, 1 means Yes, and 0 means No.

95
00:08:50,152 --> 00:08:55,623
If any of the three is yes, choose: Yes, my pain is currently adequately managed.

96
00:08:55,873 --> 00:09:00,469
If all three are no, choose: Yes, my pain is currently unmanaged.

97
00:09:00,719 --> 00:09:03,815
For this patient, scheduled pain medication is 1.

98
00:09:03,855 --> 00:09:08,514
So if the patient had pain, it would count as adequately managed.

99
00:09:08,764 --> 00:09:13,317
But the pain scores show no routine pain, so the answer stays No.

100
00:09:14,619 --> 00:09:19,190
Skip the next question: Do you have a good understanding of your health conditions,

101
00:09:19,230 --> 00:09:23,396
including when to seek additional help from a healthcare provider?

102
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:27,974
For Do you have life planning documents in place?, always answer Yes.

103
00:09:28,224 --> 00:09:32,175
These patients always have life planning documents in place.

104
00:09:35,986 --> 00:09:41,212
The next question, What is your recommended diet?, is the last one you answer here.

105
00:09:41,462 --> 00:09:47,935
The assessment's sections are listed on the left. After this question, you'll move on to the next section.

106
00:09:59,552 --> 00:10:04,361
To find the patient's diet, open the patient's page in PointClickCare.

107
00:10:10,762 --> 00:10:14,675
Click Expanded, at the top right of the patient's details.

108
00:10:15,086 --> 00:10:19,481
The expanded view shows more about the patient, including the diet.

109
00:10:22,819 --> 00:10:28,518
This patient's diet is NAS/LCS: no added salt and low concentrated sweets,

110
00:10:28,558 --> 00:10:33,090
with regular texture and regular (thin) consistency.

111
00:10:38,719 --> 00:10:41,950
In ThoroughCare, find the options that match.

112
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:47,649
Why would a patient be on low concentrated sweets? Because it's a diabetic diet.

113
00:10:47,916 --> 00:10:50,162
So check Diabetic diet.

114
00:10:53,186 --> 00:10:56,633
And no added salt means a low sodium diet.

115
00:10:56,883 --> 00:10:59,367
So check Low sodium diet too.

116
00:11:00,512 --> 00:11:08,457
If the patient eats a regular diet, with regular texture and consistency, and no restrictions, choose Other diet.

117
00:11:09,786 --> 00:11:14,275
That's the last question you answer. Skip the rest of the questionnaire.

118
00:11:14,525 --> 00:11:20,273
Skip the Goals section too, and the same goes for Barriers, Symptoms, and Interventions.

119
00:11:20,523 --> 00:11:23,359
The next section you complete is Support.

120
00:11:24,786 --> 00:11:26,310
Let's recap.

121
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:34,413
Skip the first question. Answer the ADL question from the latest MDS, printed as the MDS Form.

122
00:11:34,613 --> 00:11:42,025
06 means independent. Any other code means the patient needs assistance, so check that activity.

123
00:11:42,225 --> 00:11:48,976
Here, walking, feeding, toileting, bathing, grooming, dressing, and transferring all need assistance.

124
00:11:49,176 --> 00:11:55,671
Skip instrumental activities. Answer falls from J1800 in the MDS.

125
00:11:55,871 --> 00:12:03,859
Answer pain from the pain history in Weights and Vitals, and use J0100 to tell managed from unmanaged.

126
00:12:04,059 --> 00:12:10,426
Always answer Yes for life planning, and match the diet from the expanded view in PointClickCare.

127
00:12:12,626 --> 00:12:15,698
That's part two. The questionnaire is done.

128
00:12:15,898 --> 00:12:21,731
In part three, you add the patient's providers and support contacts in the Support section.
